/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  The declared property system.  It says WHICH components, WHICH formulation
  and WHICH models -- and nothing else.  Three things people expect to find
  here and which live elsewhere, each for the same reason (one home):

    * the CHEMISTRY (which equilibria exist) -- it comes with the components
      and the records in constant/chemistry/;
    * WHICH SOLID PHASES are admitted -- constant/chemistryDict;
    * the REFERENCE RUNG of each phase -- it is a consequence of the
      property method, not an independent choice.  Selecting the formulation
      settles the activity model, the standard states and the Henry treatment
      TOGETHER.  This is the one-knob rule, and it is why there is no
      per-component reference-state field anywhere in this case.

  Classification (from canonical component FACTS, never from names):
      water        declared aqueous solvent      (a_w-based VLE)
      CO2          molecular reactive            (aqueousSpeciation carbonate)
      NH3          molecular reactive            (aqueousSpeciation ammonia)
      H2S          molecular reactive            (aqueousSpeciation sulfide)
      aceticAcid   molecular reactive            (aqueousSpeciation aceticAcid)
      N2           molecular condensable,        (aqueousSpeciation none)
                   nonionising -- soluble, never ionising
      ethanol      molecular condensable,        (aqueousSpeciation none)
                   nonionising
      benzene      molecular condensable,        (aqueousSpeciation none)
                   nonionising
      CaCO3        nonvolatile solid             (calcite / aragonite)
      NH4HCO3      nonvolatile solid             (REFUSES -- see its record)

  MASTERS.  Five, one per family the feed reaches: Acetate (acetic), HCO3
  (carbonate), HS (sulfide), NH4 (ammonia), Ca (calcium).  Each is the
  MAJORITY species of its family at the pH this case reaches -- a master that
  is a vanishing minority (CO3-2, NH3(aq)) conditions the solve badly.  H+
  is shared by every family and is what couples them: this is where the
  acid-base competition between the ammonia and the two acids emerges,
  without any reaction being written for it.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

recordType    thermophysicalPropertySystem;
schemaVersion 2;

components    ( CO2  CaCO3  H2S  N2  NH3  NH4HCO3  aceticAcid  benzene  ethanol  water );

equilibrium
{
    formulation electrolyteGammaPhi;

    aqueous
    {
        // composite mixed-solvent electrolyte v1: Davies ions (water-referenced
        // molality) + the curated NRTL pairs on the molecular backbone -- a
        // COMPOSITE with proven limits, not an integrated Gibbs surface: no
        // ion-molecule cross terms, K's water-referenced (the mixed-solvent
        // transfer term is a NAMED gap, docs/design/standard-state-transfer-adr.md).
        activityModel { ionic davies;  molecular NRTL; }
    }

    //  THE SECOND LIQUID.  20 kmol/h of benzene against 60 of water: they do
    //  not mix, and this case exists to make the engine say so.
    //
    //  Declaring it is not optional bookkeeping.  WITHOUT this block the
    //  system has one liquid, and that liquid would swallow the benzene as
    //  though water and benzene were miscible -- a silently wrong answer the
    //  moment the case's remaining data debt is paid.  WITH it, the engine
    //  refuses by name until the solver carries the phase.  A refusal is the
    //  correct output here; a number would not be.
    //
    //  members is EXPLICIT.  What a phase admits is a modelling decision the
    //  author owns, and the exclusion that matters is not the ~0.2 mol% of
    //  water benzene really holds -- it is that the water-benzene
    //  IMMISCIBILITY which CAUSES the split is declared here rather than
    //  computed, so ethanol's partition rests on two binaries with no ternary
    //  term.  Stated in `reason`, announced on every run.
    organic
    {
        solvent  benzene;
        members  ( benzene  ethanol );
        activityModel NRTL;          // must MATCH the aqueous backbone: the
                                     // coupling is an equality of ACTIVITY
        reason
            "pedagogical organic phase: only ethanol is admitted beside the
             benzene.  The immiscibility that causes the split is declared,
             not computed, and no ternary parameter prices it.";
    }

    vapour    { fugacityModel idealGas; }

    volatiles ( CO2  H2S  N2  NH3  aceticAcid  benzene  ethanol  water );
}

/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  THE NAMED GAP OF THIS DRAFT -- the reason the case exists.

  A SECOND LIQUID PHASE (benzene-rich, admitting ethanol) has no slot in this
  grammar: `equilibrium` declares ONE liquid surface plus a vapour.  Designing
  that slot is what this draft is for, and until it exists the case CANNOT
  RUN.  Writing a placeholder here that quietly collapsed the organic phase
  into the aqueous one would be the exact failure the grammar was rebuilt to
  prevent -- a hidden default filling a slot the grammar lacks.

  When the slot is designed it must also carry the approximation HONESTLY.
  The exclusion that matters is NOT the ~0.2 mol% of water benzene really
  holds; it is that water-benzene IMMISCIBILITY -- the thing that CAUSES the
  split -- would be declared rather than computed, leaving ethanol's partition
  to two independent binaries (ethanol-water, ethanol-benzene) with no
  ternary term.  A tie-line from that is an arithmetic coincidence between
  two pairs, not a tie-line of the ternary.  And ethanol is a co-solvent:
  above ~40 mol% it closes the miscibility gap altogether.  At the ~9 mol%
  of this feed two phases still exist, but the model does not know why.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
